## a b s t r a c t
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) display promising potential due to their exquisite structural advantages. Carboxylate-based MOFs, such as MIL-53 structures, attract a lot of attention among MOF families because of their remarkable stability in water and even alkaline condition. Hence, the delicate hierarchical microstructure is constructed by introducing $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ into $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(NiFe) using a straightforward solvothermal strategy. The NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode manifests a superior OER performance, producing an overpotential of $239\mathrm{mV}$ at $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a decent Tafel slope of $87.0~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, in a typical electrodeposition equipment, NiFeMo-MOF/NF is applied as the working electrode and the composite electrode named as (M) Ni-NiOOH/NF is generated by electrodeposition and electrooxidation process to assess HER performance, producing an overpotential of $119\mathrm{mV}$ at $50\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and a decent Tafel slope of $58.3~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ . The integrated electrolysis device delivers an extraordinarily low cell voltage of $1.50\mathrm{V}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ while applying NiFeMo-MOF/NF as the anode, (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF as the cathode for overall water splitting, exceeding the noble $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}||\mathsf{P t}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}$ (1 $.60\mathrm{V}@10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ). This study provides a promising design strategy for future electrolysis catalysts.
