## 1. Introduction
The development of modern industry is continuously accompanied by the excessive usage of fossil fuels. Rechargeable metal-air batteries, fuel cells, and water electrolyzers are desirable alternative new energy and imperative to be investigated to address rapidly increasing energy consumption and severe environmental challenges [1]. The electrocatalytic water-splitting process, which involves the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), is a critical step in realizing the above clean energy technologies. However, the sluggish kinetics and large overpotential of OER and HER process impede the efficiency of overall water electrolysis [2]. As benchmarking material, precious metals such as Pt or Ir exhibit a prominent activity to catalyze HER or OER and thus accelerate the overall water splitting to produce hydrogen, but its application is impeded owing to scarce reserves and high cost [3,4]. As a result, it is particularly important to reasonably design cheap, easily available, stable, and highly active electrocatalysts based on non-precious elements.
To date, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a long-range ordered material made up of organic ligands and metal atoms [5]. MOFs, as star material, exhibit remarkable advantages including plentiful gas channels, self-assemble structures, uniform metal distribution, and long-term stability, which have attracted a surge of research in many fields, particularly in energy-related field $[6-$ 8]. In OER application, numerous MOF families have been demonstrated to possess excellent OER performance, such as Prussian blue analogs, carboxylate-based MOFs, and porphyrin-based MOFs. According to the recent research, the carboxylate-based MOFs own obvious advantages towards OER [9], including high stability (especially when it comes to combining with high-valence metal ions) [10], beneficial electronic structure (such as plentiful unsaturated metal sites, rich oxidized metal structures) [11], and flexible coordination configuration (the representative MOF structure such as MIL-88B [12], MIL-74 [13], MOF-101 [14], and MIL-53 [15]). In addition, the exposed carboxyl groups favorably enhance hydrophilicity and accessibility of hydroxyl ions, allowing the intermediates’ adsorption strength to be adjusted. So far, several efforts have been carried out to regulate the electron structure of carboxylate-based MOFs to construct efficient electrocatalysts with various microstructures, such as three-dimensional (3D) hierarchical microstructures. For example, Zha et al. reported a hierarchical microstructure (Co, Fe-MOF-74/Co/CC-2), which exhibited excellent electrical conductivity and superhydrophilic property [16]. Chen et al. reported a simple strategy to synthesize a hierarchical MOF electrocatalyst: ${\mathsf{C o N i F e O}}_{\mathrm{{x}}}{\mathrm{{\Omega}}}$ -NC, which shows a highly efficient OER performance with a low overpotential of $265~\mathrm{mV}$ at $50\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ [17]. Although the carboxylate-based MOFs have a lot of advantages, their OER performance is still restricted by the insufficient electrical conductivity and high charge transfer resistance [18]. Therefore, to eliminate the negative effect and improve the insufficient conductivity of the carboxylate-based MOF materials, it is feasible and of great significance to grow directly on substrates with excellent conductivity, for example nickel foam (NF). For instance, Sun et al. synthesized NiFe-based MOF nanosheets on NF surface by using solvothermal method, which showed excellent OER performance with an overpotential of $233~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach current density of $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [19]. The carboxylate-based MOF nanosheets tended to develop vertically on the surface of NF and form a porous frame structure, promoting the conductivity and intrinsic activity of the catalyst.
To construct carboxylate-based MOF with enhanced electrocatalytic performance, linking two or more transition metals with organic carboxylate ligand to generate bimetallic or polymetallic MOF is a conventional strategy [20]. For example, the NiFe-based
MOF catalysts, it has been extensively reported due to favorable charge transfer kinetics, high conductivity, and excellent intrinsic catalytic activity [21]. However, synthesis of the carboxylate-based MOF with a uniform distribution and stable structure remains a major challenge. As a result, doping high-valence metals (W, Mo, Nb, Ta, Ra, etc.) to MOF materials has recently been proven as a promising strategy to prepare efficient and stable electrocatalysts. Wang et al. [22], for example, described a $\mathsf{M o O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf{N i}$ -MOF catalyst with excellent HER performance, which exhibited a low overpotential of $58\mathrm{mV}$ at $20\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and the synergistic impact of Mo doping was found crucial to efficient and stable performance. Among the high-valence metals, Mo (mostly in the form of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ ) gained popularity in researchers because of its anisotropic growth characteristics [23]. In addition, the species containing $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ can undergo productive etching-reconstruction steps with resoluble $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ leaching. During the OER process, the MOFs dissociate and reform into metal hydroxides and oxyhydroxides, acting as real active centers [24]. Due to in-situ self-reconstruction triggered by electrooxidation occurs mainly on the structure’s surface, the active sites are buried in the core of the MOF structure during the OER process, thus restrict the OER activity [25]. Liu et al. reported the NiFebased layered double hydroxides intercalated with resoluble molybdate $(\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-})$ anions, which was demonstrated as an ideal catalyst for achieving complete self-reconstruction due to dissemble $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ component leaching [26]. To our knowledge, however, OER electrocatalysts based on Mo-doped NiFe-based MOF have seldom been published. It is significant to explore the effects of introducing Mo into MOF structure on OER activity.
According to the literature, most of carboxylate MOFs or derivatives can achieve good OER performance through the structure regulation, but the HER performance remains very poor. MOFs hybridized with active species are a promising strategy to synthesize MOF-based mixed component composites, which results in synergistic effects and shows outstanding performance compared to a single component [27]. For example, Yan et al. prepared the ternary NiFeMoS nanorods, which possessed scaly surfaces and showed efficient overall water splitting [28]. Yang et al. [29]developed a MOFderived chromium-doped nickel sulfide as an active OER electrocatalyst, in which ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ transformed into NiOOH during water oxidation process to generate highly electrocatalytic active sites. Given the above methods, it provides a feasible strategy to synthesize the efficient carboxylate-based MOF HER catalyst.
In this work, the molybdate incorporated NiFe-MOF was synthesized on the NF substrate (termed as NiFeMo-MOF/NF) as the efficient OER catalyst. The distinctive hierarchical microstructure of NiFeMo-MOF/NF provided abundant active sites and boosted the OER performance. Besides, the superhydrophilic performance of NiFeMo-MOF/NF accelerated the mass and charge transfer at the electrocatalyst-electrolyte interface, contributed to improve the catalytic activity of the electrocatalyst [30]. On account of the NiFeMo-MOF/NF showed a restrictive HER performance, the NiFeMo-MOF/NF hybrid Ni-NiOOH nanosheet (termed as (M) NiNiOOH/NF) was prepared through electrodeposition and electrooxidation and applied as the HER catalyst. Furthermore, the NiFeMo$\mathsf{M O F/N F}||(\mathbf{M})\mathbf{Ni}\mathbf{-Ni}{\mathbf{OOH}}/\mathbf{NF}$ couple was conducted to evaluate overall water splitting (with a low cell voltage of $1.50\mathrm{V}$ to reach current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ ), which is one of the decent values among previously described overall water splitting electrocatalysts.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Material
Nickel $(\mathbb{T})$ chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $98\ \%$ ), Iron (III) nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $98\ \%$ , Sodium molybdate dihydrate $(\mathrm{Na_{2}M o O_{4}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ $99\ \%$ ), absolute ethanol and acetone $(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{C H O}$ , $99\ \%$ ) were purchased from Sinopharm chemical reagent Co. Ltd. Acetic acid glacial $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH},99\mathrm{\%}\right)$ was purchased from Tianjin Fuyu Fine Chemicals Co. Ltd. Potassium hydroxide (KOH, $90\ \%$ was purchased from Rhawn chemical reagent Co. Ltd. Ruthenium oxide $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ , $75\mathrm{\ut\%}$ , Nafion solution $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and platinum on carbon (Pt-C, $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ was purchased from Suzhou Sinero Technology Co. Ltd. 2-amino terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , HPLC, $98\ \%$ ) was purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Nickel foam (NF, $1\mathrm{mm}$ thickness) was purchased from Zhengzhou Jinghong Corporation. All reagents used were of analytical grade and used as received without further purification.
## 2.2. Sample preparation
The NF ( $1\mathrm{cm}\times2\mathrm{cm};$ was ultrasonically treated for $15\mathrm{min}$ with a $3.0\mathrm{M}$ HCl solution, acetone, and deionized water, respectively, to remove the surface oxide and organic remains. The NF was then dried in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of OER electrode
$\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(0.2\mathrm{\mmol})\mathrm{}^{\cdot}$ ), ${\mathsf{N i C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{(0.1~mmol)}$ ), and $\mathsf{N a}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\left(0.1\ \mathrm{\mmol}\right).$ were dissolved in a mixed solution $(32.5~\mathrm{mL})$ ) composed of ethanol, acetic acid glacial, and pure water (the volume ratio was 30:0.5:2). After vigorously stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}–{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ $0.3\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) was added to the mixed solution and continuously stirred for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mixed solution was transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, and two pieces of NF were immersed into the solution. Subsequently, the autoclave was sealed and heated at $135^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After natural cooling, the obtained brown–red NiFeMo-MOF/NF was first ultrasonically treated for 2 s and rinsed consecutively with deionized water and ethanol, and then dried at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The NiFeMo-MOF loading was about $2{\mathrm{~mg~cm}}^{-2}$ .
For comparison, NiFe-MOF/NF was prepared using the identical procedure of NiFeMo-MOF/NF except for the absence of ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{MoO}}_{4}.$ - $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . The Ni-MOF/NF was synthesized under the same condition as the NiFeMo-MOF/NF without the addition of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{Na_{2}M o O_{4}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ .
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of HER electrode
The HER electrode was prepared through an electrodepositionelectrooxidation process based on NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode. First, KOH $(0.0281\mathrm{~g},\ 0.1\mathrm{~mmol})$ and ${\mathsf{N i C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ ( $12.324\ \mathrm{~g},$ $0.0104~\mathrm{mol}^{\cdot}$ ) were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water to configure an electroplating bath. Next, the as-prepared NiFeMo-MOF/NF was acted as the working electrode, Ag/AgCl electrode and graphite rod were used as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The electrodeposition was performed at a cathode current density of $36\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ (vs. $\mathsf{A g/A g C l})$ with a deposition time of $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the electrodeposition temperature was $30^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . Finally, the electrode prepared via electrodeposition was electrochemically oxidized for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ at $1.55~\mathrm{V}$ (vs. RHE) in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution. The electrode was rinsed and dried, denoted as (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF.
For comparison, Ni-NiOOH/NF was prepared using the identical procedure, with the exception that the NF acted as the working electrode.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of $R u O_{2}/N F$ and Pt-C/NF
To prepare a homogeneous suspension, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ commercial Pt-C or $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ was well-dispersed in diluted Nafion alcohol solution $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol, $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ deionized water, and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion). Then, $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ homogeneous suspension was dropped into NF $(1\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm})$ ) electrode and dried in air. As a result, the mass loading of $\mathtt{R u O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{P t-C}$ was around $2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ .
## 2.3. Materials characterization
The feature of crystal structure was observed by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Rigaku Smart lab SE) with a Cu ka X-ray source $\?\lambda=1$ . 5406 Å). The morphology and microstructure was revealed by using a field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Tescan mira3) and a high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HR-TEM, FEI Tecnai G2F20). The content and distribution of elements in samples were investigated by energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS, Oxford x-max 20) equiped on an SEM. The chemical state of the sample was revealed by using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific K-Alpha) with Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\P}$ radiator, employing a power of $12{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textperthousand~}}}$ and a beam spot of $400~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . XPSPEAK4.1 software was used to deconvolute the measured signals, employing a Tougaard background correction and Gaussian/Lorentzian peak shapes. The binding energy was calibrated using C 1s $(284.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ . FT-IR spectra were recorded on Bruker spectrometer in the wavenumber range of $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Physical adsorption instrument (Micromeritics ASAP 2460) was used to investigate the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm. Contact angle device (JGW-360B) was used to characterize the static water contact angle using the sessile drop method with a water droplet volume of $5~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ at environment temperature.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical experiments were performed in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH using a standard three-electrode system on Gamry electrochemical workstation (Interface1000). The prepared electrode (working area around $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ ), Saturated calomel electrode (SCE), and graphite rod were used as working electrode, the reference electrode, and counter electrode, respectively. To assess the electrocatalytic performance, linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was recorded with a scan rate of $2{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ at room temperature. $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}=\mathrm{E}_{(\mathrm{SCE})}+0.059\times$ $\mathsf{p H}+0.242\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ equation was used to calibrate the LSV to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). As for the OER investigation, the overpotential $(\eta)$ was calculated according to the equation: $\eta=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}$ $\mathrm{~-~}1.23~\mathrm{V}.$ In addition, the overpotential $(\eta)$ for HER investigation was obtained according to the equation: $\eta=-\ \mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}$ . Furthermore, the Tafel slope curves were derived by fitting the LSV data using the equation $\eta=\mathtt{a}+b\log|j|$ , where $\eta$ is the overpotential, $b$ is the Tafel slope and $j$ is the current density. $95\%$ iR – compensation was used to correct the potential recorded in this work. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were operated with such parameters: frequency range of $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ , and a $5\mathrm{mV}$ amplitude perturbation. To extract the critical kinetics parameter, a suitable equivalent circuit model was used to fit the obtained EIS data. The double-layer capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{dl}})$ was investigated by Cyclic voltammetry under the potential window of $1.24\mathrm{~V~}$ to $1.29{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. RHE) for OER and $0.15{\mathrm{~V~}}$ to $_{0.2\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. RHE) for HER at various scan rates of $10{-}50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , respectively, to estimate the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) of the electrocatalyst. The reproducibility of the results was verified by repeated measurements for at least three times.
## 3. Results and discussion
The NiFeMo-MOF/NF was synthesized via solvothermal process, and the electrodeposition-electrooxidation process was employed to introduce the Ni-NiOOH layer to activate HER performance, as illustrated in Scheme 1.
The obvious color change was observed after the growth of NiFeMo-MOF structure on the NF substrate, as shown in Fig. S1. The NF and Ni-MOF/NF exhibited silver with a metallic shine, while the NiFeMo-MOF/NF displayed a dark yellow. It was worth noting that the NiFeMo-MOF/NF turned into bright yellow after the introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ , suggesting the surface morphology and structure had altered.
The crystallinity and phase of the NiFeMo-MOF/NF were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD). Three prominent diffraction peaks at $44.5^{\circ}$ , $51.8^{\circ}$ , and $76.4^{\circ}$ in NiFeMo-MOF could be ascribed to the (111), (200), and (220) crystal planes of Ni (PDF#04-0850) (Fig. S2), respectively. Except for three pronounced diffraction peaks of Ni, which ascribed to the strong signal of NF substrate, no other distinct diffraction peaks were observed. Hence, the NiFeMo-MOF powder was scratched off from the NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode and further explored by XRD. The XRD pattern (Fig. 1a) is compared with the simulated XRD pattern of MIL-53(NiFe) [19], the as-prepared sample is isostructural to MIL-53(NiFe), indicating the successful synthesis of MOFs.
Fig. 1b shows the FT-IR spectra of NiFeMo-MOF, NiFe-MOF, NiMOF. The absorption peaks at $3410~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1380~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $751~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in those samples can be associated with the stretching vibration of the $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ , symmetric and asymmetric vibrations of the carboxyl groups, $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending vibrations of the benzene rings, respectively [31]. The presence of $\nu_{s}$ $\:(-\mathsf{C O O-})$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{as}}$ $(-\mathsf{C O O-})$ adsorption bands connecting to the metal atom reveals that there is no free $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC ligand in the NiFeMo-MOF [17]. Significantly, the FT-IR spectra of NiFeMo-MOF and NiFe-MOF powder is quite similar, indicating that they possess the same characteristic bonding structure [10]. The increasing peak signal of NiFeMo-MOF at $621~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , compared to Ni-MOF, is attributable to $\nu$ (FeAO), indicating that a metal–oxygen bond is formed between the carboxyl group of organic ligand and the Ni(Fe) atom [32].
The morphology and microstructure of the NiFeMo-MOF/NF, NiFe-MOF/NF, Ni-MOF/NF was examined by SEM, as demonstrated in Fig. 2 and Fig. S3. The NiFeMo-MOF/NF possesses a hierarchical microstructure, as shown in Fig. 2a-e. From the low magnification images in Fig. 2a-b, dozens of micron-sized spheres are observed evenly covered on the NF substrate, each microsphere is composed of nanosheet aggregation. From the higher magnification image shown in Fig. 2c-e, we can find that each nanosheet, in turn, is made up of more delicate nanopine needle-like structures, nanoscale pine needles grow perpendicular to micron-scale branches to form a biomimetic hierarchical microstructure. This delicate hierarchical microstructure would make the solid surface rougher, and bring special wettability to the electrocatalyst according to the wetting theory of the solid surface [33]. In contrast, NiFeMOF/NF and Ni-MOF/NF exhibit a rather insipid microstructure, nano-sheets and micron-sheets are observed randomly distributed on the NF, as shown in Fig. S3. Consequently, the unique microstructure of NiFeMo-MOF/NF can be ascribed to the anisotropic growth characteristics of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ [34,35], indicating the successful introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ into the MOF structure. In addition, EDS mapping (Fig. 2f) image reveals that Ni, Fe, and Mo elements are homogeneously distributed across the hierarchical microstructure, confirming that the sample contains welldispersed catalytic active sites.
TEM analysis was conducted to confirm the biomimetic hierarchical microstructure of NiFeMo-MOF/NF. From the TEM image of NiFeMo-MOF (Fig. 3a), the hierarchical microstructure can be observed. Fig. 3b reveals that the nanosheet is composed of more delicate nanopine needle-like structures, which is consistent with SEM images of NiFeMo-MOF/NF (Fig. 2a-e). The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) inset in Fig. 3b exhibits two distinct diffraction rings, which correspond well with the (1 3 4), (2 3 7) facets of MIL-53(NiFe) [19]. The high-resolution TEM images of NiFeMoMOF are depicted in Fig. 3c-d, some fine lattice fringes are dispersedly distributed in the location with yellow mark (Fig. 3d).
The wettability of NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode was examined and compared with NF electrode, as shown in Fig. 4. NiFeMoMOF/NF electrode displays superhydrophilic property, the water droplet permeates into the electrode once the droplet contacts with the surface of the sample, the static contact angle is close to zero. Nevertheless, the NF electrode possesses a static water contact angle of $100.6^{\circ}$ , exhibiting a hydrophobic nature. The superhydrophilic performance of NiFeMo MOF/NF electrode originated from NiFeMo-MOF hierarchical microstructure endows better infiltration ability of the electrolyte, hence accelerates the mass and charge transfer at the electrocatalyst-electrolyte interface, predictively improves the catalytic activity of the electrocatalyst.
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm of as-prepared electrocatalysts is depicted in Fig. S4. Fig. S4a-b all exhibit a similar type Ⅳ isotherm, confirming the existence of mesoporous structure [36]. The BET surface area of NiFeMo-MOF/NF ( $\bar{\cdot}10.65\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1};$ is greater than that of NiFe-MOF/NF $(8.83\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\mathrm{~.~}$ and Ni-MOF/NF $(5.33\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , implying the NiFeMo-MOF/NF possesses greater contact area and more active sites under OER reaction. It is worth mentioning that, during a continuous redox reaction, the porous structures can not only extend the volume to expose the catalytic active sites, but also promote the rapid diffusion of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ and electrons, resulting in excellent OER activity [37].
From the above discussion, the instruction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ significantly modifies the morphology of NiFeMo-MOF/NF, which can provide a larger surface area, expose abundant active sites, boost the mass and charge transfer, and facilitate the penetration of electrolytes. Moreover, the superhydrophilic performance of NiFeMoMOF/NF electrode promotes the diffusion of aqueous electrolyte and ensures the rapid release of bubbles, contributing to the excellent electrocatalytic performance [38].
The elemental composition and electronic state of NiFeMoMOF/NF were further revealed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The XPS survey spectrum in Fig. 5a reveals the presence of Ni, Fe, Mo, C, O, and N elements, the atomic ratio of Mo/Ni and $\mathrm{Fe/Ni}$ is near to 0.06 and 0.33 (Table S1), respectively, implying successful doping of Fe and Mo. Fig. 5b shows the highresolution spectrum of Ni 2p, the peaks at the binding energy of $856.5\mathrm{eV}$ and 874.3 eV are assigned to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, as well as satellite peaks at $861.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $879.7\mathrm{eV}$ confirming the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ oxidation state. In the Fe 2p spectrum (Fig. 5c), the binding energy peaks at $712.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $724.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ along with two satellite peaks at $717.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $731.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , are assigned to Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, suggesting the presence of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ . The binding energy of $705.7\mathrm{eV}$ is identified as the Ni LMM auger peak.
In the Mo 3d spectrum of NiFeMo-MOF/NF, the peaks at $232.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $235.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to $\mathrm{Mo^{6+}3d_{5/2}}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , implying the introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ [39]. With the combination of the FT-IR result (Fig. 1b), the presence of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ in the MOF structure as a free state is further confirmed. As shown in Fig. S5a, the C1s spectrum of NiFeMo-MOF/NF reveals three types of peaks: the benzoic rings of $N{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{-H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}$ linkers at $284.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , organic ligand carboxylate groups at $288.4\ \mathrm{eV},$ , and the $c{-}0$ at $286.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ [19]. As for the O 1s (Fig. S5b), the primary peaks at 531.3 eV, 532.3 eV, and $534.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to metal–oxygen bonds, carboxylate groups of the organic ligand, and absorbed water, respectively [21]. Furthermore, as for the N 1s (Fig. S5c), the peak at $399.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to the $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ group of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ linkers. All of those results imply that the NiFeMo-MOF/NF was successfully synthesized through the solvothermal method.
The OER performance of the as-prepared electrode was investigated via linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in 1.0 M KOH solution at room temperature. The NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode (Fig. 6a) exhibits the lowest overpotential of $239\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much lower than the overpotential of NiMOF/NF $285\mathrm{mV},$ ), NiFe-MOF/NF $265\mathrm{mV},$ ), $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}\left(430\mathrm{mV}\right)$ , and blank NF. In addition, Tafel plots were derived from the LSV curves to assess the reaction kinetics during OER. Fig. 6b shows that the NiFeMo-MOF/NF exhibits enhanced reaction kinetics for OER, as evidenced by its lower Tafel slope $(87.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ) than that of Ni-MOF/NF $'140.9\ \mathrm{\mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), NiFe-MOF/NF $(98.6~\mathrm{\mV~dec^{-1}},$ , $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ ( $112.0\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), NF $\mathrm{'}198.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ). Comparison of the overpotential of MOF-based catalysts at $50~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ or $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ is shown in Fig. 6c. The NiFeMo-MOF/NF only needs an overpotential of $265~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which shows the best electrocatalytic performance compared to NiFe-MOF/NF and Ni-MOF/NF. Fig. 6d presents the Nyquist plots of various electrodes at 1.49 V (vs. RHE), which reveals the kinetics of the mass and charge transfer of electrocatalyst [40]. According to the fitted values of series impedance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ and charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ inserted in Fig. 6d, the NiFeMo-MOF/NF has the lowest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value $\left(0.93~\Omega\right)$ when compared to the other catalysts (Table S2), suggesting an extremely accelerated electron transfer rate can be realized by $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ introducing. The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ was estimated to evaluate the ECSA because of the proportional relation between ECSA and $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ , which can further identify the possible mechanism for the improved catalytic activity after the introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ [41]. The CV measurement with different scan rates was conducted to obtain the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ in the potential range of $1.24{-}1.29\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. S6). The $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ was calculated by plotting $\Delta\mathrm{j}=(\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{a}}-\mathrm{j}_{\mathrm{c}})/2$ against the scan rate at 1.265 V (vs. RHE). The NiFeMo-MOF/NF exhibits the highest $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ value $(23.65~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\$ in Fig. 6e, which is much higher than that of NF $(2.47\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ ), Ni-MOF/NF $\mathrm{:10.31mF~cm^{-2}},$ , NiFe
MOF/NF $\cdot12.87~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{6.61\mathrm{\mF\cm}^{-2}}$ ). Combined with the above-mentioned characterization results, the enhanced performance is benefited from the structure of the biomimetic hierarchical microstructure and remarkable superhydrophilic property, which provides a large and effective electrocatalystelectrolyte contact interface area. Additionally, the electrocatalyst’s long-term stability towards OER is critical for an energy conversion system. The long-term chronopotentiometry (v-t) measurement was carried out to estimate the durability of the NiFeMo-MOF/NF catalyst. Only $1.36\ \%$ potential decline (Fig. 6f) is detected after $40\mathrm{~h~}$ stability test. After the durability measurement, the obtained LSV curve almost the same with the initial one (Fig. S7). Besides, SEM images after $40\mathrm{~h~}$ stability test are provided in Fig. S8, demonstrating that the morphology of hierarchical microstructure is preserved during the stability test, providing a guarantee for the long-term durability of NiFeMo-MOF/NF during OER catalysis. It can be predicted that the excellent OER performance and durability of NiFeMo-MOF/NF can be associated with the coupling effects of delicate hierarchical microstructure, remarkable intrinsic catalytic activity, favorable charge transfer kinetics, and superhydrophilic property of the electrode.
To further investigate the OER mechanism of NiFeMo-MOF/NF, the NiFeMo-MOF/NF after stability test was assessed by XPS. The XPS survey spectrum (Fig. 7a) confirms the existence of Ni, Fe, O, K, and C elements in the NiFeMo-MOF/NF after stability test. The intensity of Mo 3d in Fig. 7b is greatly reduced after stability test, suggesting the loss of Mo elements in NiFeMo-MOF/NF. Meanwhile, Mo signal receding further reveals the complete dissolution of NiFeMo-MOF/NF and $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ leaching after deep selfreconstruction via electro-oxidation [26]. The XPS spectrum of Ni 2p (Fig. 7c) exhibits decreased binding energy $(\sim1.0\mathrm{eV})$ after stability test, demonstrating an increase of electron density due to the destruction of the metal-organic ligand, thus facilitating the formation of ${\mathrm{Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ [42]. Additionally, two minor peaks at $856.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ and 874.6 eV are attributed to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ , which are associated with the creation of NiOOH during the OER reaction. Although Fe 2p spectrum after stability test (Fig. 7d) is nearly identical to the initial one, the strong synergetic effect between Fe and Ni is beneficial to the generation of active oxy-hydroxide, thereby enhancing the catalytic [43]. The decreased peaks of C 1s (Fig. 7e) and N 1s (Fig. S9) after stability test indicate the dissolution of organic ligand. Fig. 7f shows the O 1s spectrum, the peaks at 530.4 eV, 531.2 eV, $533.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ after the stability test can be attributed to the MAO, MAOH, and $\scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}-0$ , respectively. The new existence of the MAOH bond further demonstrates the formation of Fe/Ni oxyhydroxide during the OER process [44]. As discussed above, the reconstruction caused by $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ leaching promotes the metal binding with $0\mathrm{H}^{-}$ from the electrolyte, resulting in hydroxides and oxyhydroxides, which improves the OER performance.
The excellent OER performance of NiFeMo-MOF/NF is attributed to the introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ and the Ni/Fe binding mode of carboxylate ligands. (1) The biomimetic hierarchical microstructure of NiFeMo-MOF/NF provides better infiltration ability for the electrochemical process, which is favorable for the interfacial charge transfer during OER. (2) The introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ increases BET specific surface area of NiFeMo-MOF/NF, which creates a high density of catalytically active sites available for surface reaction. (3) The $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ enhances reconstruction and enable $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ invasion, which promotes the generation of actual active species during the OER procedure and improves OER performance. (4) The strong synergetic effect between Fe and Ni is beneficial to the generation of active oxy-hydroxide, thereby enhancing the electrocatalytic activity.
Based on NiFeMo-MOF acting as OER electrode, the construction of NiFeMo-MOF hybridized Ni-NiOOH electrode was synthesized by electrodeposition and electro-oxidation. The peaks of NiFeMo-MOF after electrodeposition are closely matched with simulated MIL-53(NiFe), Ni (PDF#04–0850), and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ (PDF#38–0715) in the XRD pattern (Fig. S10), indicating the existence of NiFeMo-MOF, Ni, and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ after electrodeposition.
After electro-oxidation, the XRD pattern of the sample revealed the conversion of ${\mathrm{Ni}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ to NiOOH, and the disappeared peak signal at $8.9^{\circ}$ demonstrated the dissolution-reconstruction of NiFeMoMOF. Fig. S11 depicts the morphology of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF, nanosheets aggregated to form microspheres decorated on the NF substrate. No obvious hierarchical microstructure is observed in the SEM image of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF, further confirming the NiFeMo-MOF structure is dissolved and reconstituted. The LSV curves of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF were investigated to evaluate HER activities. For comparison, the NF, NiFeMo-MOF/NF, Ni-NiOOH/ NF, Pt-C/NF electrodes have also been investigated. The LSV curves (Fig. 8a) suggest that the (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF outperforms the NF,
NiFeMo-MOF/NF and Ni-NiOOH/NF. As expected, the (M)NiNiOOH/NF exhibits a high HER activity, requiring overpotential of $119~\mathrm{mV}$ and $165~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , respectively, to obtain $50\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mA}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-2}$ $(j_{50})$ and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}\ (}$ $(j_{100})$ , which is slightly higher than Pt-C/NF $(j_{50}=70~\mathrm{mV}$ , $j_{100}=112\mathrm{mV}$ . Moreover, the (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF exhibits a small Tafel slope $(58.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}.$ ), which is the lowest among the as-prepared catalysts except for the commercial Pt-C/NF $(56.0\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}},$ ) (Fig. 8b). In comparison with Ni-NiOOH/NF and NiFeMo-MOF, (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF has a lower Tafel slope, indicating that NiFeMo-MOF was hybridized with Ni-NiOOH to construct a heterogeneous structure and accelerate the HER process. The overpotential of as-prepared HER catalysts at different current densities is provided in Fig. 8c, which indicates the (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF possess an excellent HER performance.
To further understand HER kinetics, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) experiments were performed at a suitable potential ( 0.27 V vs. RHE). According to the fitting data extracted from the EIS curves, the (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF has the lowest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of 0.07 $\Omega$ compared to Ni-NiOOH/NF $\left(0.89\Omega\right)$ , NiFeMo-MOF/NF $\left(2.78\Omega\right)$ , NF $(4.58~\Omega)$ and Pt-C/NF $\left(0.40\Omega\right)$ (Fig. 8d and Table S3), demonstrating (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF has the fastest charge transfer capability and highest electrocatalytic efficiency. Meantime, this result indicates that the NiFeMo-MOF precursor can significantly improve electron-transport characteristics. To further analyze the improved electrocatalytic performance offered by NiFeMo-MOF, the electrochemical active surface areas were calculated from the $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ based on CV measurement at a scan rate from $10\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ to $50\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ (Fig. S12). Fig. 8e shows that the (M)Ni
NiOOH/NF exhibits a remarkably larger $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ $(50.31~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) than Ni-NiOOH/NF $26.41\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}.$ ), indicating the electrochemically active area can be enhanced greatly by applying the NiFeMoMOF/NF as the deposition substrate. In addition to the high activities, (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF possesses robust stability during HER process. As shown in Fig. 8f, the catalyst presents no obvious degradation upon the long-term galvanostatic test. After the $40\mathrm{~h~}$ stability test, the polarization curve of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF is nearly identical to the initial one (Fig. S13), suggesting the excellent stability of the (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF electrode.
Considering the excellent OER performance of NiFeMo-MOF/NF and outstanding HER performance of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF, twoelectrode configuration with the NiFeMo-MOF/NF as anode and (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF as the cathode was conducted to investigate overall water splitting activity in 1.0 M KOH solution. In addition, the couple $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}||\mathsf{P t}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}$ and the couple NF||NF were also investigated for comparison. As shown in Fig. 9a, the couple NiFeMo-MOF/NF||(M)Ni-NiOOH/NF exhibits a low operating voltage of $1.50\mathrm{V}$ and $1.66\mathrm{V}$ to output a water-splitting current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $50\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. Such performance is remarkably superior to the $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}||\mathsf{P t}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}$ (1.60 V $@$ $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and NF||NF $1.83\mathrm{~V~@~}10\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}},$ , confirming the NiFeMo-MOF/NF||(M)Ni-NiOOH/NF couple is a viable replacement for noble metal catalysts towards overall water splitting. Fig. 9b and Table S4 show the NiFeMo-MOF/NF||(M)Ni-NiOOH/NF possesses the lowest $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $62.58\ \Omega,$ compared to $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}||\mathsf{P t}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{N F}$ $(758.20\Omega)$ and NF||NF $(4729\Omega)$ . In further comparison with previously described non-noble metal based overall water splitting electrocatalysts, our work demonstrates one of the best performance (Fig. 9c). Moreover, water electrolyzer durability is evaluated at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , and the electrolysis process of NiF $\mathsf{a M o-M O F/N F}||(\mathbf{M})\mathsf{N i-N i O O H/N F}$ can be sustained for at least $40\mathrm{~h~}$ with the potential decline of only $1.85\%$ . After the durability measurement, the obtained LSV curve is slightly deviates than the original one (Fig S14). Meanwhile, vigorous gaseous bubbles on the electrode’s surface could be observed during the experiment, as shown in Fig. 9d. These results emphasize great promise for practical application of the NiFeMo-MOF/NF||(M)Ni-NiOOH/NF as highly efficient water splitting electrocatalyst.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, the extremely efficient and stable NiFeMo-MOF/ NF OER electrocatalyst was prepared by using a simple solvothermal method. The NiFeMo-MOF/NF electrode shows excellent OER performance with required overpotential of $239~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve the current density of $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a Tafel slope of $87.0~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ in 1.0 M KOH, outperforming most of the non-noble metal catalyst described in the recent literature. Besides, this electrode exhibits superior long-term durability with Only $1.36\ \%$ potential decline for $40\mathrm{h}$ . The excellent OER performance is mainly attributed to the hierarchical microstructure originated from the introduction of $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ , large surface area, superhydrophilic characteristics, and strong synergetic effect between Fe and Ni. In addition, the NiFeMo-MOF/NF hybridized Ni-NiOOH nanosheets were synthesized by electrodeposition and electro-oxidation, which exhibits outstanding HER performance $119~\mathrm{\mV}$ $@$ $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH. Inspired by the excellent OER performance of NiFeMo-MOF/NF and outstanding HER performance of (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF, the NiFeMo-MOF/NF and (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF were assembled as the anode and cathode, respectively, to evaluate the overall water splitting activity. The integrated electrolysis device delivers an ultra-low cell voltage of $1.50\mathrm{V}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , demonstrating a high level of overall water splitting activity. This newly developed NiFeMo-MOF/NF or (M)Ni-NiOOH/NF electrode and the corresponding design strategy provide a promising way to develop stable and high-efficiency electrocatalysts and its application in advanced power system.
